\par
     Fresh, warm air, redolent with spices and the scent of fecund soil and sun-warmed incense -- Reese shuddered at the first whiff of Inu-case. She'd been born to recirculated air on Mars; from there she'd gone to the Earthrise. The freedom of the evening breeze struck her as unnatural and the varied smells alarming.\par
\par
     "And there's where we're headed," Sascha said, pointing out the first of the buildings as they crested the hill.\par
\par
     "How far are we from the jail?" Reese said, choosing the moment to stop for breath. Inu-Case's heavier gravity had sapped much of her energy on the twenty-minute walk from the Earthrise's position. They hadn't wanted to land too close to town, just in case. Still, she envied Kis'eh't, whom she'd left to guard the ship.\par
\par
     "Once we hit the buildings, we'll be two blocks south of it," Sascha said, studying his data tablet. The tip of his tiger-striped tail peeked from beneath his brown overcoat. "They didn't want it too close to the center of town, I imagine."\par
\par
     Irine squinted. "Looks pretty quiet down there. I guess we picked a good time to come by."\par
\par
     "Let's just hope someone's there to take our credit and let him out," Reese said. "Come on."\par
\par
     Rising above the streets in the orange glow of the street lamps, the wooden houses had an ominous cast. What few glimpses of the surrounding land Reese could catch between the houses revealed only a crimped plain drowned in violet shadows and the black smudges of distant mountains. The few trees dotting the lawns proved the source of the odor: their round, waxy leaves reeked so badly that the two felines took to skirting them, and even Bryer seemed discomfited by them.\par
\par
     In sunlight, perhaps the rustic building materials and open streets would have seemed inviting. Reese couldn't shake the sense of unease that seeing them in the dark aroused. It didn't help that there was no one outside. No children playing. No people walking home. No one talking, wandering the streets. Reese had never been to a slaver's retreat, but she could only imagine them all being this silent, as if everyone was afraid to call attention to herself.\par
\par
     "Doesn't anyone live here?" Irine asked in a whisper.\par
\par
     Bryer glanced into one of the buildings. "Deserted."\par
\par
     "Really well-maintained for someplace deserted," Sascha said, tail lashing.\par
\par
     "I don't like this at all," Irine said. "It looks like a pirate hide-out."\par
\par
     "Try not to look rushed, people," Reese said. "If anyone's watching, we have business here and we're not worried about it."\par
\par
     "Let's just hope they can't hear us talking," Sascha muttered.\par
\par
     At the corner they stopped to allow a single sparrow zip past . . . a peculiar conveyance in a town, overpowered for mere hops across blocks and underpowered for any serious spaceflight. Reese watched it streak past and pressed a hand to her stomach.\par
\par
     "Angels! I can't decide whether to be glad there are actually people living here or not," Irine muttered.\par
\par
     "Nevermind the people," Sascha said, pointing at an unprepossessing one-story building. "That's our stop."\par
\par
     "Let's get this over with," Reese said, and marched to the door.\par
\par
     "How come there's no gate? You know, with electrified wire or stunner fields or something?" Irine asked.\par
\par
     "I don't know," Reese said. She tried one of the doors--it was locked. "I guess all their guards are on the inside." She scanned for a bell and found none. "Are you sure this is the front?"\par
\par
     "I can check around the sides," Sascha said.\par
\par
     "Do that," Reese said. "Take Bryer with you."\par
\par
     "Yes ma'am."  \par
\par
     "And in the mean time we just stand here," Irine said. "While security cameras stare at us."\par
\par
     "We're not doing anything wrong," Reese said.\par
\par
     "Right," Irine said, tail whipping nervously. When it smacked Reese one too many times on the calf, Reese hissed, "Stop that."\par
\par
     "Sorry."\par
\par
     They waited. And waited.\par
\par
     "It can't be that big a place, can it?" Irine said.\par
\par
     "No," Reese said, feeling a headache beginning to knot her brows. "Something's happened."\par
\par
     "Should we go look?" Irine asked.\par
\par
     She wanted to say 'no,' so of course she said, "Yes." And, "Keep close." Then she set off around the perimeter of the jail. The nearest buildings were still set far enough away that they could see anyone coming. No gates or traps startled them.\par
\par
     "I can still smell him," Irine said. She tapped on the wall. "But it stops right here."\par
\par
     Reese glanced at the grass surrounding the featureless wall, then bent and examined it. Irine joined her a moment later. They stared together.\par
\par
     "See anything?"\par
\par
     "No," Reese said.\par
\par
     Irine's ears flipped back. "Would you know it if you saw something?"\par
\par
     "I haven't exactly done much investigative policework in my life," Reese said. She sighed and straightened. "Let's go back around front."\par
\par
     There were no other doors. No lights. Nothing. By the time they wound back up around to the forbidding doors, Reese was beginning to get angry. She pointed at the door. "Do something about that."\par
\par
     For once, Irine did not protest innocence, but began scrutinizing the doors and feeling along their edges. Reese watched her with growing impatience, but forced herself to remain still until the mysterious actions of the Harat-Shar bore fruit.\par
\par
     "Not bad. But not up to specs," Irine commented as she pocketed her electronic picks and pushed the doors. They complained with a faint creak, then inched open. The girl peeked in through the crack. "Doesn't seem to be manned," she whispered.\par
\par
     Reese glanced behind her shoulder -- still nothing. Not a person walking up the dusky streets, no sound of music or laughter or life, just the constant slough of the perfumed breeze. In front of her, a sealed door with no guards and not a breath of a living person, despite Sascha and Bryer having vanished without a clue. There were people here, people far more dangerous than she was.\par
\par
     "Maybe we should go," Reese said.\par
\par
     "But my brother!" Irine whispered. "And Bryer!"\par
\par
     "We need reinforcements," Reese said.\par
\par
     "What, Kis'eh't and Allacazam? They'll help," Irine said, scowling.\par
\par
     "I was thinking more like Fleet," Reese said. "We're not up to this, Irine."\par
\par
     "Think what you want," Irine said. "I'm not leaving Sascha in there."\par
\par
     "Irine--!"\par
\par
     But the girl had already slipped inside. Reese lunged after her, trying to catch her tail, but Irine was already ghosting past the empty front desk to the row of cells. No halo shield arced across the wall leading to them; no guards stood rigidly before them. Reese fought a renewed foreboding as she hurried after the Harat-Shar. The warmth of the stone floor communicated to her toes through the soft material of her boots.\par
\par
     "No sign of Sascha or Bryer," Irine called back, "but at least here's our expensive Eldritch!"\par
\par
     Reese sprinted after the girl, a cold sweat erupting on her brow. As she pushed Irine aside, her throat closed for a precious second at the sight of the sleeping human. Then, strangled, "That's not him!" She flung herself around, preparing to flee--\par
\par
     And met the business end of a metal pipe. She didn't even remember going down.\par
